From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: offer the picture settings as V4L2 controls

The picture pipeline of this chip belongs to the MIPS co-processor, so
every picture setting it has is a THal_Vp_* remote procedure and not a
register the ARM could write. That has meant a private tool so far: the
values were reachable only by sending RPCs over /dev/cpu_comm by hand, and
nothing that looks for picture settings where V4L2 keeps them -- v4l2-ctl,
qv4l2, any ordinary application -- could find them at all.

Offer them on /dev/videoN, where the pictures come out.

Nine of them. Brightness, contrast, saturation, hue and sharpness take the
standard ids. The two noise reductions, the dynamic contrast and the black
extension have no standard id in V4L2 at all, so they take ids from a new
user-class block for this driver; their four steps and the names of those
steps are the vendor's own, out of the header of pq_picturemode.ini
("0-off,1-low,2-middle,3-high"), which is also where the 0..100 range of
the other five comes from.

That these RPCs reach *our* picture is measured, not assumed. SetContrast
0 -> 100 moves 12.16 % of the pixels on the wall and the smaller step
20 -> 80 moves 0.84 % of them, on the picture this driver captures
(doku/nachtlog/K5-board-verifikation.md (c) and (d)).

No value is converted on the way in. Each of the five settings measured on
the device lands unchanged in its field of the firmware's register block --
SetContrast 20/80/100 gives 0x14/0x50/0x64 in 0x05001234[31:16] -- so the
V4L2 range is mapped onto the RPC argument and onto nothing else.
Saturation shows why that is the right place to stop: the same RPC also
writes a second register, the chroma gain at 0x05140508[23:16], as
floor(argument * 1.28), and a driver that computed a register value itself
would get that half wrong.

The controls start at the values the firmware reports, not at values picked
here. After the bring-up sequence the driver asks each setting's Get
routine once and uses the answer as the control's value and as its default.
That is what makes a truthful control possible without inventing a number:
announcing 50 while the firmware holds something else is a lie, and writing
50 to make the announcement true would move a picture nobody asked to move
-- for saturation it would take the chroma gain with it. An answer outside
the range, or none at all, falls back to the vendor's "standard" preset and
says so in the log.

The readback checks itself. The bring-up sequence has just set the temporal
and spatial noise reduction, the dynamic contrast and the black extension
to known values, and the driver compares its four answers against them --
reading the expected values out of the sequence table rather than writing
them down a second time, so the two cannot drift apart. Four agreements are
evidence that the Get routines are read correctly; a disagreement is a
warning in the log and means this patch's reading of them is wrong.

Both call shapes come from the decompiled handlers
(re/captures/weltneuheit/k5-pq-handler-20260907.log): a Set handler reads
its value out of the first argument word and touches nothing else, a Get
handler takes no argument at all and answers with exactly one word, the
value. That matters for more than convenience -- SetGamma, SetWhiteBalance
and SetColorManagement take a *pointer* that the firmware dereferences
without checking it, the failure mode that takes the co-processor down when
THal_Vp_Init is handed a zero staging address. None of those three is
offered here.

Nothing is offered twice either. Gamma and white balance are already
GAMMA_LUT and CTM on the display CRTC, where they are measured to invert
the picture on the console and on the video path alike, while the firmware
announces "Can not get MP GAMMAModuleID" for its own gamma module at every
start; the lamp already has pwm-backlight and a backlight class device to
go with it. Picture mode, video range and low latency mode are left out for
reasons the code names one by one -- in short, because their values cannot
be named from anything the vendor ships, and a menu whose entries cannot be
named is worse than no menu.

Brightness was the doubtful one of the nine and is not doubtful any more.
It writes its register -- SetBrightness 100 gives 0x00000064 in
0x05001234[15:0] -- but the run that looked for an effect on the wall saw
0.00 % of the pixels move, where the same step in contrast moved 12.16 %,
and the conclusion drawn from that was to leave the control out. The run
went against a nearly white page, and brightness moves the lower end of the
curve: its criterion could not have succeeded whatever the hardware did.
Re-measured against dark material: the standard deviation in the region of
interest goes 12.6 -> 16.2 -> 22.7 for SetBrightness 0 -> 50 -> 100 and
then stays flat through 150, 200 and 255, and stepping back and forth
lands on the same three numbers again
(doku/nachtlog/I0-helligkeit-nachgemessen.md). So brightness works -- and
its range really is 0..100, which is now measured and not only read off
the vendor file.

v4l2_ctrl_handler_setup() is deliberately not called: it would write all
nine values into the firmware during probe, which is exactly what the
readback exists to avoid.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c

Nachtrag der Hauptsitzung, 07.09.2026 12:40. Der Entwurf fragte beim Probe
THal_Vp_Get<x> ab und nahm die Antwort als Ist- und Vorgabewert. Am Gerät ist
das durchgefallen, und zwar an der Selbstprüfung, die der Entwurf selbst dafür
eingebaut hatte: "0 von 4 gegen die Init-Sequenz bestaetigt". Die Gegenprobe
gegen die Register zeigt, dass die Rückfrage falsch liegt und nicht die
Erwartung -- 0x05001248[7:0] hält SNR 1 und 0x0500123c[7:0] hält DCI 2, beide
von der Init-Sequenz gesetzt, und beide Get-Rufe antworten trotzdem 0. Auch
unmittelbar nach einem erfolgreichen SetContrast 80, mit 0x50 sichtbar im
Register, liefert GetContrast 0. Die Get-Routinen sind damit als Quelle
unbrauchbar; sie sind ersatzlos raus, und die Startwerte kommen aus der
Init-Sequenz beziehungsweise dem Ruhewert der Register. Hergang:
doku/nachtlog/I1-get-routinen.md.

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
